# full-fidelity-in-both-understanding-and-validation

Shortcuts in comprehension and shortcuts in validation produce the same failure mode: the system appears correct while a real violation goes undetected. Full-fidelity understanding (read all plans, trace all paths, understand all interfaces) and full-fidelity validation (assert on real content, validate all coupled layers) are two expressions of a single discipline: never accept a partial view as a complete one. Gaps in either phase are silent — they feel like green until the production failure reveals them.

## Procedures
- Treat both the pre-implementation comprehension pass and the post-implementation assertion suite as complete-coverage requirements — partial is not acceptable in either.
- When reviewing a PR or test suite, ask: 'Would this pass if the real invariant silently broke?' If yes, the coverage is insufficient.
- When building a mental model of the system, ask: 'Is there any path, plan version, or coupling I have not traced?' If yes, continue reading before acting.
- Surface any gap (unread plan, unchecked layer, proxy assertion) explicitly before committing — do not paper over it.

